      "source_line_start": 3128,
      "source_line_end": 3144,
      "current_tex": "\\begin{theorem}[Ambient Existence of Supremum]\n\\label{thm:ambient-existence-supremum}\nThere exist a linearly ordered set $(P,\\leq)$ and subsets\n$A\\subseteq S\\subseteq S'\\subseteq P$ such that $A$ has a supremum relative to\n$S'$ but has no supremum relative to $S$.  For example, in $\\mathbb{R}$, let\n\\[\n  A=S=(0,1),\n  \\qquad\n  S'=(0,1].\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n  \\sup_{S'}A=1,\n\\]\nwhereas $A$ has no upper bound in $S$, and hence no supremum relative to $S$.\n\\hyperref[prf:ambient-existence-supremum]{Go to proof.}\n\\end{theorem}",
      "formalizations": []
    }
  },
  "creation_mode": "prompt"
